================================================================================
## Lakebase companion table: fail fast when the graph schema is not writable
================================================================================

Context:
A managed-synced Build failed at "Creating companion table" with
"permission denied for schema public". The Postgres graph schema existed but
was owned by another role (the Lakebase project owner), and the app service
principal had no USAGE/CREATE on it. Postgres silently drops such a schema
from the search_path, so the unqualified CREATE TABLE fell through to public
and the error named the wrong schema. The Build now checks access to the graph
schema first and reports the real cause with the fix.

Changes:

1. src/back/core/graphdb/lakebase/_companion_ddl.py
   New ensure_schema_writable(cur, schema): checks has_schema_privilege USAGE
   and CREATE on the graph schema and raises InfrastructureError naming the
   schema and the remediation (scripts/bootstrap/lakebase-perms.sh -s <schema>
   or make bootstrap-lakebase). Called by _create_triple_table right after
   CREATE SCHEMA IF NOT EXISTS, so both the companion (managed_synced) and the
   _sync table (app_managed) paths are covered.
2. tests/units/core/test_companion_ddl_schema_access.py
   New tests: privilege query shape, error text, and no CREATE TABLE when the
   schema is not writable.
3. tests/units/core/test_lakebase_flat_store.py
   Legacy-migration fixture answers the new schema-access query first.

Modified files:
- src/back/core/graphdb/lakebase/_companion_ddl.py
- tests/units/core/test_companion_ddl_schema_access.py (new)
- tests/units/core/test_lakebase_flat_store.py
- changelogs/v0.9.0/simyung-yang_2026-10-27.log

